1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,273
Things break.

2
00:00:01,273 --> 00:00:05,973
What separates a beginner from an administrator is not never breaking anything.

3
00:00:05,973 --> 00:00:07,400
It is finding the cause,

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,649
and fixing it calmly.

5
00:00:09,199 --> 00:00:11,405
Today you learn a four step method.

6
00:00:11,405 --> 00:00:15,123
Then you use it on four faults: a script that will not run,

7
00:00:15,123 --> 00:00:16,383
a disk that is full,

8
00:00:16,383 --> 00:00:18,462
a service nobody is listening to,

9
00:00:18,462 --> 00:00:20,101
and a broken service file.

10
00:00:20,651 --> 00:00:23,310
Every fault is a real recording on Ubuntu,

11
00:00:23,310 --> 00:00:24,449
broken on purpose,

12
00:00:24,449 --> 00:00:26,539
so you can watch the method work.

13
00:00:28,589 --> 00:00:30,765
Here is the method. It has four steps.

14
00:00:31,315 --> 00:00:32,602
One: read the symptom.

15
00:00:32,602 --> 00:00:33,772
What exactly failed,

16
00:00:33,772 --> 00:00:35,352
and what did the error say?

17
00:00:35,352 --> 00:00:36,229
Copy the words.

18
00:00:36,229 --> 00:00:36,990
Do not guess.

19
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:42,254
Two: check. Ask the system what is true, with a command, before you change anything.

20
00:00:42,804 --> 00:00:47,839
Three: fix one thing. Make the smallest change, and keep a backup of any file you edit.

21
00:00:48,389 --> 00:00:49,249
Four: verify.

22
00:00:49,249 --> 00:00:51,234
Run the original action again.

23
00:00:51,234 --> 00:00:52,027
If it works,

24
00:00:52,027 --> 00:00:52,887
you are done.

25
00:00:52,887 --> 00:00:53,350
If not,

26
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:54,740
you have a new error,

27
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:56,261
so go back to step one.

28
00:00:56,811 --> 00:01:01,312
Slow is smooth. Change five things at once, and you never learn which one worked.

29
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:08,077
Fault one. A script will not run. Type: ./backup.sh.

30
00:01:08,627 --> 00:01:11,295
Read the symptom: permission denied.

31
00:01:11,295 --> 00:01:12,630
The file is there,

32
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:15,447
but the system will not let us run it.

33
00:01:15,447 --> 00:01:17,300
So check the permissions.

34
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:18,931
Type: ls -l backup.sh.

35
00:01:19,481 --> 00:01:21,079
The mode is dash r w,

36
00:01:21,079 --> 00:01:21,611
dash r,

37
00:01:21,611 --> 00:01:22,372
dash dash,

38
00:01:22,372 --> 00:01:22,524
r,

39
00:01:22,524 --> 00:01:23,285
dash dash.

40
00:01:23,285 --> 00:01:24,503
Nobody has an x,

41
00:01:24,503 --> 00:01:26,405
so nobody may execute it.

42
00:01:26,405 --> 00:01:27,623
We own the file,

43
00:01:27,623 --> 00:01:29,145
so we can change it.

44
00:01:30,095 --> 00:01:31,348
Fix one thing.

45
00:01:31,348 --> 00:01:34,392
Add the execute bit for the owner.

46
00:01:34,392 --> 00:01:36,719
Type: chmod u+x backup.sh.

47
00:01:36,719 --> 00:01:37,973
U is the user,

48
00:01:37,973 --> 00:01:40,121
and plus x adds execute.

49
00:01:40,671 --> 00:01:46,047
Verify. Run the original command again. Type: ./backup.sh.

50
00:01:46,597 --> 00:01:47,172
It runs.

51
00:01:47,172 --> 00:01:48,753
Fixed with one change.

52
00:01:48,753 --> 00:01:50,765
Not chmod seven seven seven,

53
00:01:50,765 --> 00:01:53,424
which would give everyone everything.

54
00:01:55,474 --> 00:02:03,005
Fault two. You try to save a file, and it fails. Type: echo hello > mnt/new.txt.

55
00:02:03,555 --> 00:02:05,722
No space left on device.

56
00:02:05,722 --> 00:02:06,264
Check,

57
00:02:06,264 --> 00:02:07,439
do not guess.

58
00:02:07,439 --> 00:02:08,884
Type: df -h mnt.

59
00:02:08,884 --> 00:02:12,045
D F shows how full a filesystem is.

60
00:02:12,595 --> 00:02:14,922
Use is one hundred percent.

61
00:02:14,922 --> 00:02:17,680
Next question: what is using it?

62
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:19,146
Type: du -ah mnt,

63
00:02:19,146 --> 00:02:21,214
then a pipe to sort -rh,

64
00:02:21,214 --> 00:02:22,507
then head -n 3.

65
00:02:22,507 --> 00:02:25,523
That lists the three biggest items.

66
00:02:26,473 --> 00:02:30,988
Two lines show eighty-eight megabytes: the folder total,

67
00:02:30,988 --> 00:02:32,681
and old dump dot log.

68
00:02:32,681 --> 00:02:34,616
The file is the culprit.

69
00:02:34,616 --> 00:02:36,793
Check it is safe to remove.

70
00:02:36,793 --> 00:02:38,969
Then delete only that file.

71
00:02:38,969 --> 00:02:41,065
Type: rm mnt/old-dump.log.

72
00:02:42,015 --> 00:02:42,736
Verify.

73
00:02:42,736 --> 00:02:45,826
Try the original action again.

74
00:02:45,826 --> 00:02:49,019
Type: echo hello > mnt/new.txt,

75
00:02:49,019 --> 00:02:49,431
and,

76
00:02:49,431 --> 00:02:49,842
and,

77
00:02:49,842 --> 00:02:50,975
echo saved.

78
00:02:51,525 --> 00:02:55,899
Saved. Confirm with D F. Type: df -h mnt.

79
00:02:56,449 --> 00:03:01,249
The space is back. D F says how full. D U says what is using it. Use both.

80
00:03:03,299 --> 00:03:04,566
Fault three.

81
00:03:04,566 --> 00:03:09,105
A web server on port 8080 is not answering.

82
00:03:09,105 --> 00:03:10,583
Test the port.

83
00:03:10,583 --> 00:03:13,538
Type: nc -zv 127.0.0.1 8080.

84
00:03:13,538 --> 00:03:15,438
Z means just test,

85
00:03:15,438 --> 00:03:17,550
and v means tell me.

86
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:19,506
Connection refused.

87
00:03:19,506 --> 00:03:22,836
That is a precise clue: the machine answered,

88
00:03:22,836 --> 00:03:25,870
but no program is listening on that port.

89
00:03:25,870 --> 00:03:29,422
A silent hang would point at a firewall instead.

90
00:03:29,422 --> 00:03:30,384
Double check.

91
00:03:30,384 --> 00:03:32,308
Type: ss -tln | grep 8080.

92
00:03:33,158 --> 00:03:34,301
Nothing printed.

93
00:03:34,301 --> 00:03:36,732
Nothing is listening on port 8080.

94
00:03:36,732 --> 00:03:38,734
So the fault is the service,

95
00:03:38,734 --> 00:03:39,878
not the network.

96
00:03:40,828 --> 00:03:43,986
Fix one thing: start the service.

97
00:03:43,986 --> 00:03:48,675
For this demo it is Python's built in web server.

98
00:03:48,675 --> 00:03:53,268
Type: python3 -m http.server 8080 &>/dev/null &.

99
00:03:53,268 --> 00:03:57,574
The last ampersand runs it in the background.

100
00:03:58,524 --> 00:04:02,404
The number in brackets is the job.

101
00:04:02,404 --> 00:04:06,626
The second number is the process I D.

102
00:04:06,626 --> 00:04:07,425
Verify.

103
00:04:07,425 --> 00:04:10,620
Type: nc -zv 127.0.0.1 8080.

104
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:18,530
Succeeded. Now the port answers. See who is listening. Type: ss -tln | grep 8080.

105
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:21,078
A listener on port 8080.

106
00:04:21,078 --> 00:04:23,074
Refused means nothing listens.

107
00:04:23,074 --> 00:04:25,803
Timed out means something blocks the way.

108
00:04:25,803 --> 00:04:27,134
Read the difference,

109
00:04:27,134 --> 00:04:28,931
and you know where to look.

110
00:04:30,981 --> 00:04:31,734
Fault four.

111
00:04:31,734 --> 00:04:33,446
A service will not start.

112
00:04:33,446 --> 00:04:38,171
On a live system you would run systemctl status and read the journal.

113
00:04:38,171 --> 00:04:40,568
Here we check the unit file itself.

114
00:04:40,568 --> 00:04:42,074
Type: cat web.service.

115
00:04:42,624 --> 00:04:44,466
A unit file has sections.

116
00:04:44,466 --> 00:04:47,192
The Service section says what to run.

117
00:04:47,192 --> 00:04:49,844
Now ask system D to verify the file.

118
00:04:49,844 --> 00:04:52,864
Type: systemd-analyze verify web.service,

119
00:04:52,864 --> 00:04:56,768
and keep only the lines about our file with grep web.

120
00:04:57,318 --> 00:04:58,607
There is the cause,

121
00:04:58,607 --> 00:04:59,624
in plain words.

122
00:04:59,624 --> 00:05:02,608
The command is not executable: no such file.

123
00:05:02,608 --> 00:05:04,304
Look closely at the path.

124
00:05:04,304 --> 00:05:05,118
Pythn three.

125
00:05:05,118 --> 00:05:06,406
A missing letter o.

126
00:05:07,356 --> 00:05:13,948
Check the real path before you edit. Type: ls /usr/bin/python3.

127
00:05:14,498 --> 00:05:15,479
It exists.

128
00:05:15,479 --> 00:05:16,852
Fix one thing,

129
00:05:16,852 --> 00:05:18,225
with a backup.

130
00:05:18,225 --> 00:05:21,952
Type: sed -i.bak and the substitution,

131
00:05:21,952 --> 00:05:23,620
then web.service.

132
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:29,602
Dash i dot bak keeps the original as web dot service dot bak.

133
00:05:30,152 --> 00:05:36,296
Verify. Run the same check again. Type: systemd-analyze verify web.service.

134
00:05:36,846 --> 00:05:37,500
Silence.

135
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:38,318
No errors.

136
00:05:38,318 --> 00:05:39,544
With this tool,

137
00:05:39,544 --> 00:05:41,097
silence is success.

138
00:05:41,097 --> 00:05:41,506
Last,

139
00:05:41,506 --> 00:05:43,877
see exactly what you changed.

140
00:05:43,877 --> 00:05:47,065
Type: diff web.service.bak web.service.

141
00:05:47,915 --> 00:05:52,566
One word changed, and you can prove it. That is how careful administrators work.

142
00:05:54,616 --> 00:05:59,565
On a live system, with system D running, you add a few more tools to the same method.

143
00:06:00,115 --> 00:06:05,214
Systemctl status shows whether a service is running, and its last log lines.

144
00:06:05,764 --> 00:06:10,351
Journalctl dash u, then the service name, shows that service's own log.

145
00:06:10,901 --> 00:06:16,682
After you edit a unit file, run systemctl daemon dash reload, so system D reads it again.

146
00:06:17,232 --> 00:06:21,541
Then restart the service, and run status again to verify.

147
00:06:22,091 --> 00:06:22,965
Same method.

148
00:06:22,965 --> 00:06:23,548
Symptom,

149
00:06:23,548 --> 00:06:23,985
check,

150
00:06:23,985 --> 00:06:24,276
fix,

151
00:06:24,276 --> 00:06:24,786
verify.

152
00:06:24,786 --> 00:06:27,262
These screens are an illustration,

153
00:06:27,262 --> 00:06:28,427
not a recording.

154
00:06:30,477 --> 00:06:31,800
Four myths to clear up.

155
00:06:32,350 --> 00:06:34,661
Myth one: a reboot fixes everything.

156
00:06:34,661 --> 00:06:34,854
No.

157
00:06:34,854 --> 00:06:36,074
It hides the cause,

158
00:06:36,074 --> 00:06:37,294
wipes the evidence,

159
00:06:37,294 --> 00:06:38,899
and the fault comes back.

160
00:06:39,449 --> 00:06:43,202
Myth two: chmod seven seven seven fixes permissions.

161
00:06:43,202 --> 00:06:43,780
It does,

162
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:45,728
by removing all protection.

163
00:06:45,728 --> 00:06:48,110
Add only the bit that is missing.

164
00:06:48,660 --> 00:06:49,964
Myth three: disk full,

165
00:06:49,964 --> 00:06:51,385
so delete something big.

166
00:06:51,385 --> 00:06:52,096
Check first.

167
00:06:52,096 --> 00:06:54,762
The biggest file may be the one that matters.

168
00:06:55,312 --> 00:06:57,844
Myth four: keep restarting until it works.

169
00:06:57,844 --> 00:07:00,317
A restart without a fix just fails again.

170
00:07:00,317 --> 00:07:01,221
Read the error.

171
00:07:01,771 --> 00:07:04,502
Read it. Check it. Fix one thing. Verify.

172
00:07:06,552 --> 00:07:08,877
Here is how you check your own understanding.

173
00:07:09,427 --> 00:07:11,198
Name the four steps of the method.

174
00:07:11,748 --> 00:07:15,588
Read a file mode with ls dash l, and fix it with chmod.

175
00:07:16,138 --> 00:07:19,253
Find a full disk with df, and what fills it with du.

176
00:07:19,803 --> 00:07:23,877
Tell connection refused from timed out, and check listeners with ss.

177
00:07:24,427 --> 00:07:29,654
Verify a unit file with systemd-analyze, and name the status and journal commands.

178
00:07:30,204 --> 00:07:33,425
Verify after every fix, and back up before you edit.

179
00:07:33,975 --> 00:07:35,597
To pass, you need all six.

180
00:07:37,647 --> 00:07:38,274
Your turn,

181
00:07:38,274 --> 00:07:40,031
in your own virtual machine.

182
00:07:40,031 --> 00:07:42,478
Break things only on a machine you own,

183
00:07:42,478 --> 00:07:44,047
and keep notes as you go.

184
00:07:44,597 --> 00:07:49,952
One: make a script, take away its execute bit, watch it fail, then fix it with chmod.

185
00:07:50,502 --> 00:07:55,366
Two: fill a small loop disk image, find the culprit with D U, and free the space.

186
00:07:55,916 --> 00:08:00,652
Three: start a web server, stop it, read connection refused, and bring it back.

187
00:08:01,202 --> 00:08:06,471
Four: put a typo in a unit file, and catch it with systemd dash analyze verify.

188
00:08:07,021 --> 00:08:09,386
Five: on a machine running system D,

189
00:08:09,386 --> 00:08:11,752
make a real service fail on purpose.

190
00:08:11,752 --> 00:08:13,592
Read status and the journal.

191
00:08:14,142 --> 00:08:19,667
Six: for each fault, write four lines in a notes file: symptom, check, fix, verify.

192
00:08:20,217 --> 00:08:23,524
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

193
00:08:39,074 --> 00:08:39,949
Quick check.

194
00:08:40,499 --> 00:08:42,888
Which command shows what is using disk space?

195
00:08:43,438 --> 00:08:45,358
What does connection refused tell you?

196
00:08:45,908 --> 00:08:48,340
Which tool checks a unit file for mistakes?

197
00:08:48,890 --> 00:08:50,639
What do you do after every fix?

198
00:08:51,189 --> 00:08:57,760
Objective covered: MC04-O10.

199
00:08:58,310 --> 00:09:00,379
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

200
00:09:10,929 --> 00:09:14,513
Next lesson: the Linux administration mastery scenario.

201
00:09:15,063 --> 00:09:18,391
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
